WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.300
Welcome back to Building
HR-Driven Identity.

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:08.513
This is Chapter 4: access from attributes
and the mover's residual review.

3
00:00:08.513 --> 00:00:11.624
An automatic policy removes
what its rule granted.

4
00:00:11.624 --> 00:00:14.599
It never removes what
someone granted by hand,

5
00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:18.200
and a move is exactly when
that access stops being right.

6
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:23.349
So this chapter starts from Learn's
procedure for an automatic assignment policy,

7
00:00:23.349 --> 00:00:27.968
then adds what Learn leaves to you: the
policies as reviewed files, a lint of

8
00:00:27.968 --> 00:00:32.663
nine rules, a scan for the member-of
operator, and the mover's residual review.

9
00:00:32.663 --> 00:00:35.340
Then two labs, and the dangers.

10
00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:40.677
Why does this gate need code?

11
00:00:40.677 --> 00:00:44.354
The guide's Step 4 assigns
department packages by rule,

12
00:00:44.354 --> 00:00:49.666
one automatic policy per package, with no
member-of operator, a grace period for

13
00:00:49.666 --> 00:00:54.814
movers, manager approval for requests,
and a review of what's left after a move.

14
00:00:54.814 --> 00:00:59.802
Each of those is a property of a policy
body or a fact about what a user holds.

15
00:00:59.802 --> 00:01:04.327
That means code can check it every time,
instead of someone checking it once.

16
00:01:04.327 --> 00:01:07.978
And for every rule, you'll know
whether it's Learn's contract,

17
00:01:07.978 --> 00:01:11.000
Learn's warning, or the
program's own design.

18
00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.350
Learn's page on automatic assignment
policies is the procedure.

19
00:01:18.350 --> 00:01:22.387
A policy carries a membership
rule in the dynamic group syntax.

20
00:01:22.387 --> 00:01:27.063
Entitlement management creates assignments
for users who match, removes them

21
00:01:27.063 --> 00:01:31.313
for users who stop matching, and a grace
period can keep access for a while.

22
00:01:31.313 --> 00:01:34.466
Learn suggests one automatic
policy per package,

23
00:01:34.466 --> 00:01:38.849
warns that overlap can lose users
their access, caps a rule at fifteen

24
00:01:38.849 --> 00:01:43.463
thousand identities, and needs the
Identity Governance Administrator.

25
00:01:43.463 --> 00:01:45.735
And from November third, 2026,

26
00:01:45.735 --> 00:01:51.220
a policy using member-of is quarantined:
it stays, but does nothing.

27
00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:59.599
Learn's Create assignment policies
page, example three, gives the request:

28
00:01:59.599 --> 00:02:04.607
a post to the assignment policies endpoint,
scoped to specific directory users,

29
00:02:04.607 --> 00:02:07.566
with an attribute rule
target holding the rule,

30
00:02:07.566 --> 00:02:11.057
the automatic request settings
and the access package.

31
00:02:11.057 --> 00:02:15.455
Those settings have just three
properties: create for those in scope,

32
00:02:15.455 --> 00:02:18.232
remove when they leave,
and the grace period.

33
00:02:18.232 --> 00:02:23.031
For request policies, the requestor manager
setting makes the requester's manager

34
00:02:23.031 --> 00:02:27.840
the approver, and Learn says to add another
approver in case there's no manager.

35
00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.628
Learn gives the portal
steps and one request body.

36
00:02:34.628 --> 00:02:37.973
It doesn't keep policies where
a reviewer approves them,

37
00:02:37.973 --> 00:02:41.247
test a rule against the
population before it goes live,

38
00:02:41.247 --> 00:02:45.902
find overlap between two policies, or
say what a move leaves outside the rules.

39
00:02:45.902 --> 00:02:50.898
So the kit keeps one file per policy,
turns Learn's warnings into lint rules,

40
00:02:50.898 --> 00:02:53.518
scans the tenant's export for member-of,

41
00:02:53.518 --> 00:02:58.103
refuses privileged packages by rule,
and lists the mover's residual.

42
00:02:58.103 --> 00:03:01.686
The verdict: keep Learn's body
and checkboxes as they are,

43
00:03:01.686 --> 00:03:04.140
and enforce its warnings in code.

44
00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:09.328
Here's what the kit ships.

45
00:03:09.328 --> 00:03:14.561
The config access folder holds two automatic
policies, one per department package,

46
00:03:14.561 --> 00:03:19.566
each written on the organization code and
the worker type, with a seven-day grace.

47
00:03:19.566 --> 00:03:23.963
And one request policy for infrastructure
administrator eligibility,

48
00:03:23.963 --> 00:03:26.602
which the manager
approves, with a fallback.

49
00:03:26.602 --> 00:03:30.990
It grants eligibility only; the
role is activated through PIM.

50
00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:35.003
Each file's program block records
its tier and the groups it grants.

51
00:03:35.003 --> 00:03:39.545
A small evaluator reads only the
rule operators these policies use,

52
00:03:39.545 --> 00:03:42.340
and raises an error on anything else.

53
00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:47.677
The lint has nine rules.

54
00:03:47.677 --> 00:03:49.902
Member-of in a rule is a finding.

55
00:03:49.902 --> 00:03:53.529
Two automatic policies on one
package that a user matches

56
00:03:53.529 --> 00:03:56.702
at once is a finding, and
two disjoint ones a warning.

57
00:03:56.702 --> 00:03:59.965
More than fifteen thousand
identities is a finding.

58
00:03:59.965 --> 00:04:02.958
A rule with no coded slot
or worker type warns,

59
00:04:02.958 --> 00:04:05.952
because a department
name breaks on a rename.

60
00:04:05.952 --> 00:04:08.641
A policy must both create and remove.

61
00:04:08.641 --> 00:04:11.040
A grace over fourteen days warns.

62
00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.815
Requests need the manager and a fallback.

63
00:04:13.815 --> 00:04:17.130
And no privileged package
may be assigned by rule.

64
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:25.830
The residual review takes the mover's old
and new attributes, the effective date,

65
00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:30.405
their package assignments, their group
memberships and the policy files.

66
00:04:30.405 --> 00:04:35.761
For each automatic assignment, it
re-evaluates the rule on the new attributes:

67
00:04:35.761 --> 00:04:38.355
kept, or removed at the end of the grace.

68
00:04:38.355 --> 00:04:41.467
Anything from another policy
is marked for review.

69
00:04:41.467 --> 00:04:44.795
Dynamic groups are rule-managed,
groups a held package

70
00:04:44.795 --> 00:04:48.492
grants are package-managed, and
anything else goes to review.

71
00:04:48.492 --> 00:04:50.767
The sheet goes to the manager of record,

72
00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:54.250
and the interval from transfer
to review is measured.

73
00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.600
The reads are one task.

74
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.475
Listing policies and assignments
needs entitlement management read.

75
00:05:03.475 --> 00:05:07.724
Group memberships use the transitive
call on purpose, because it needs

76
00:05:07.724 --> 00:05:12.262
only user read, while another user's
direct memberships need directory read.

77
00:05:12.262 --> 00:05:14.700
The tools read Graph
on the cloud you name.

78
00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:19.731
One gap matters here: administrative
units with dynamic rules exist

79
00:05:19.731 --> 00:05:24.762
only in global Azure, so a GCC High
tenant uses the guide's interim path.

80
00:05:24.762 --> 00:05:26.751
Nine tests cover this chapter,

81
00:05:26.751 --> 00:05:30.770
and all fifty-seven of the
kit's tests pass with no tenant.

82
00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:38.095
The verified facts, each
read on November first.

83
00:05:38.095 --> 00:05:42.358
The automatic policy is generally
available, with the limits we've seen.

84
00:05:42.358 --> 00:05:45.358
Member-of rules are quarantined
from November third.

85
00:05:45.358 --> 00:05:51.507
The create call is in version one point
oh, available in the L4 and L5 clouds.

86
00:05:51.507 --> 00:05:55.758
And dynamic administrative units
aren't available in Azure Government.

87
00:05:55.758 --> 00:06:00.760
Two places where Learn stops are recorded
so a later edit doesn't loosen the code:

88
00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.792
the fallback approver is
required, not optional,

89
00:06:03.792 --> 00:06:08.080
and overlap is found only among
the users the population holds.

90
00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:13.680
What does this give the I S S O?

91
00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:19.323
The reviewed policy files, the lint's
clean run and the hash of each body sent,

92
00:06:19.323 --> 00:06:23.692
for account management and
least privilege, AC-2 and AC-6.

93
00:06:23.692 --> 00:06:28.542
The member-of scan with zero rows, which
the guide's validate step asks for.

94
00:06:28.542 --> 00:06:31.401
A clean run of the
privileged-package rule,

95
00:06:31.401 --> 00:06:37.692
showing no privileged role reached by
rule, which SCuBA requires under BOD 25-01.

96
00:06:37.692 --> 00:06:39.957
And each mover's residual sheet,

97
00:06:39.957 --> 00:06:45.180
the manager's decisions and the
interval, for personnel transfer, PS-5.

98
00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:49.160
Separation of duties stays in
the package configuration.

99
00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:53.835
Who does what?

100
00:06:53.835 --> 00:06:56.415
Developers build the access automation;

101
00:06:56.415 --> 00:07:00.360
they read Python and JSON and
know dynamic membership rules.

102
00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.262
Entitlement management
owners keep the packages,

103
00:07:03.262 --> 00:07:07.823
and only an Identity Governance
Administrator can create an automatic policy.

104
00:07:07.823 --> 00:07:11.761
Policy reviewers approve each file
before it reaches the tenant.

105
00:07:11.761 --> 00:07:14.547
The manager of record
approves requests and

106
00:07:14.547 --> 00:07:17.335
decides each item on a
mover's residual sheet.

107
00:07:17.335 --> 00:07:21.111
And the I S S O files the
evidence: the lint runs,

108
00:07:21.111 --> 00:07:25.150
the empty scan, the
sheets and the intervals.

109
00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:32.387
Lab 1 runs on a laptop
with Python and no tenant.

110
00:07:32.387 --> 00:07:35.134
Step one lists the reads
and their permissions:

111
00:07:35.134 --> 00:07:39.922
the assignment policies and the assignments
under entitlement management read,

112
00:07:39.922 --> 00:07:42.738
and the transitive
memberships under user read.

113
00:07:42.738 --> 00:07:43.824
Nothing writes.

114
00:07:43.824 --> 00:07:47.039
Step two lints the program's
three policy files,

115
00:07:47.039 --> 00:07:50.500
evaluating each rule over
the fixture population.

116
00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:53.000
Zero findings, zero warnings.

117
00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:56.770
That's the baseline every
new policy file has to keep.

118
00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:05.233
Step three lints five broken policies,
and the lint exits with code two.

119
00:08:05.233 --> 00:08:08.021
A rule using member-of
would be quarantined.

120
00:08:08.021 --> 00:08:12.796
Two Program Operations policies
overlap on two employees of ORG-100,

121
00:08:12.796 --> 00:08:16.316
because one rule reads the
department name and the

122
00:08:16.316 --> 00:08:20.170
other matches every employee;
those two can lose access.

123
00:08:20.170 --> 00:08:25.545
One automatic policy doesn't remove, and
it also hands tier one access out by rule.

124
00:08:25.545 --> 00:08:28.433
A request policy has no fallback approver.

125
00:08:28.433 --> 00:08:34.320
And the department-name rule warns twice:
no coded slot, and a thirty-day grace.

126
00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:41.584
Step four scans an export
of the tenant's policies:

127
00:08:41.584 --> 00:08:45.870
four read, one uses member-of,
the legacy shared drives policy.

128
00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:49.227
Learn's own script runs the
same test against a tenant;

129
00:08:49.227 --> 00:08:51.058
this one runs in the pipeline.

130
00:08:51.058 --> 00:08:53.432
Step five lists a mover's residual.

131
00:08:53.432 --> 00:08:58.426
Jordan moved from ORG-100 to
ORG-210 on October nineteenth:

132
00:08:58.426 --> 00:09:01.444
eight items held, three go to review.

133
00:09:01.444 --> 00:09:04.616
A requested package and
two groups granted outside

134
00:09:04.616 --> 00:09:07.345
any package land on the
sheet for the manager.

135
00:09:07.345 --> 00:09:11.783
The one finding: the review is still
open thirteen days after the move.

136
00:09:11.783 --> 00:09:14.970
Step six: fifty-seven tests pass.

137
00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:23.507
Lab 2 uses a test tenant, never
production; the authors haven't run it.

138
00:09:23.507 --> 00:09:25.296
Export with a read-only app,

139
00:09:25.296 --> 00:09:29.846
and run the member-of scan beside
Learn's script: the lists must match.

140
00:09:29.846 --> 00:09:34.352
Create one policy from a copy of the
program's file, with a one-hour grace,

141
00:09:34.352 --> 00:09:38.470
linted first and sent by an
Identity Governance Administrator.

142
00:09:38.470 --> 00:09:41.712
Move a test user through
the pipeline, never by hand,

143
00:09:41.712 --> 00:09:45.032
with the validation kit's
snapshot before and after.

144
00:09:45.032 --> 00:09:49.471
Run the residual, give the sheet to the
test manager, and measure the interval.

145
00:09:49.471 --> 00:09:51.300
Then back everything out.

146
00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:56.075
The dangers.

147
00:09:56.075 --> 00:10:00.275
Member-of in a rule after November
third quietly stops removal;

148
00:10:00.275 --> 00:10:02.213
the lint and the scan catch it.

149
00:10:02.213 --> 00:10:05.063
Two policies a user can match lose access.

150
00:10:05.063 --> 00:10:08.649
A rule on a display name
drops everyone on a rename.

151
00:10:08.649 --> 00:10:13.925
A policy that never removes, or a long
grace, lets access outlive the move.

152
00:10:13.925 --> 00:10:17.037
Privileged access by rule
is standing privilege.

153
00:10:17.037 --> 00:10:19.779
Hand-granted access on
a mover stays forever,

154
00:10:19.779 --> 00:10:22.150
which is what the residual sheet is for.

155
00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:23.800
Never edit the hidden group.

156
00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:28.370
And a rule the evaluator can't read
raises an error instead of pretending.

157
00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:33.470
Here's what done looks like.

158
00:10:33.470 --> 00:10:36.456
Every policy in the tenant
has a reviewed file,

159
00:10:36.456 --> 00:10:39.520
and the lint is clean over
a current population.

160
00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:43.058
The member-of scan and Learn's
script both come back empty.

161
00:10:43.058 --> 00:10:48.333
Each package has one automatic policy,
or two whose disjointness is recorded.

162
00:10:48.333 --> 00:10:51.113
No privileged package is assigned by rule,

163
00:10:51.113 --> 00:10:53.894
and every request has a fallback approver.

164
00:10:53.894 --> 00:10:57.621
Every move has a residual sheet,
a decision for each item,

165
00:10:57.621 --> 00:11:00.132
and an interval within the threshold.

166
00:11:00.132 --> 00:11:04.382
Next, Chapter 5 covers
certificate-based authentication checks.

167
00:11:04.382 --> 00:11:05.550
Thanks for watching.
